<code>assert !c.isEmpty() => \forall int i; 0<=i && i<c.size(); this.contains(c.toArray()[i]);</code>

However, this assertion does not fully capture that the list should contain exactly the same elements as the collection, including the order and multiplicity. A more accurate assertion would be:

<code>assert !c.isEmpty() => \forall int i; 0<=i && i<c.size(); this.get(i).equals(c.toArray()[i]) && this.size() == c.size();</code>

But since we are only asked to check for the presence of elements and not their order or multiplicity, the first assertion is sufficient based on the given comment. However, to strictly adhere to the comment's meaning of "exactly the same elements," we should consider both the presence and count of each element. Here is the more comprehensive assertion:

<code>assert \forall Object e; true; \old(c).stream().filter(x -> x.equals(e)).count() == this.stream().filter(x -> x.equals(e)).count();</code>

Since we need to use only publicly accessible observer methods, we can simplify it to:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0<=i && i<c.size(); this.contains(c.toArray()[i]) && this.size() == c.size();</code>

But to ensure exact match including duplicates, a more precise assertion using only public methods would be:

<code>assert \forall Object e; true; \old(c).indexOf(e) != -1 => this.indexOf(e) != -1 && \old(c).lastIndexOf(e) != -1 => this.lastIndexOf(e) != -1 && \old(c).indexOf(e) == this.indexOf(e) && \old(c).lastIndexOf(e) == this.lastIndexOf(e);</code>

